'''Club Esportiu Sant Bart''' ({{lang|ca|Club Deportivo Sant Bart}}), commonly referred to as '''CE Sant Bart''' or simply '''Sant Bart''', is a professional [[association football|football]] club based in [[Sant Bart]], [[Jala Province]], [[Balboa]]. The club competes in [[La Lliga de l'Istme|La Lliga]], the top tier of the [[Balboa football league system]], and plays its home matches at the [[Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)|Estadi Nacional]].

Founded in 1934, Club Esportiu Sant Bart is the most successful club in Balboan football history, with 14 La Lliga titles. The club's support base is concentrated in the interior provinces, particularly among the Black Balboan community in and around Sant Bart. Its rivalry with [[FC Sant Cristòfor]] — the '''[[Clàssic de l'Istme]]''' — is the most attended fixture in Balboan football.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1934–1950) ===

Club Esportiu Sant Bart was founded on 12 March 1934 by a group of plantation workers and tradesmen in Sant Bart, the capital of the Balboan interior. The club was established as a sporting association — the ''club esportiu'' model, derived from the Volisanian ''club deportivo'' tradition — and was initially affiliated with the Sant Bart Tradesmen's Association, which provided the club's first ground, a cleared field behind the association's hall in the city's working-class district.

The founding of the club coincided with the construction of the [[Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)|Estadi Nacional]], a public works project completed in 1938 that provided Sant Bart with the largest stadium on the isthmus. The Estadi Nacional became the club's permanent home and the centre of the city's sporting life. The early years were characterised by informal competition against other interior clubs and occasional friendly matches against teams from the canal corridor, though travel between the interior and the corridor was difficult and matches were infrequent.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) disrupted the club's development. Sant Bart was occupied by Zong forces during the Balboa Campaign, and the city's population was subjected to occupation conditions for the duration of the war. The Estadi Nacional was requisitioned by the Zong military for use as a staging area, and the club was unable to play during the occupation. Football resumed only after the Zong withdrawal and the end of the war, and the club was rebuilt from the remnants of its pre-war squad and the players who had returned from the labour corps.

=== The segregated league (1951–1960) ===

{{main|Balboa football league system}}

The establishment of the '''Lliga de la República Balboana''' in 1951 placed Club Esportiu Sant Bart in a formal league for the first time. The club was one of the founding members of the league, which was organised by the '''Comitè de Futbol de la República''' under the rump state's authority. The league was founded on the Volisanian colonial model: no promotion and relegation, clubs organised as social clubs associated with the planter elite, and — under the '''Reglament de Jugadors''' — a restriction of three Black players per first-team squad and two Black players on the pitch at any time.

The Reglament de Jugadors placed CE Sant Bart in a difficult position. The club had been founded by and for the Black working class, and its support base was overwhelmingly Black, but the regulations required it to field a majority-white team. The club's board, which was composed of white members of the planter class who had assumed control of the club's administration, complied with the regulations and selected the squad accordingly. The Black players who were permitted to play were subjected to racial abuse from the stands, discriminatory treatment by referees, and the constant threat of being dropped if they challenged the conditions.

Despite the restrictions, CE Sant Bart was the dominant club in the Republic league, winning five titles between 1951 and 1960. The club's advantage was its stadium — the Estadi Nacional was the largest and best-maintained ground in the league — and its community support, which produced the largest and most passionate crowds in the interior. The Black working class filled the '''graderies''' — the open terraces behind the goals — every matchday, and the atmosphere at the Estadi Nacional was the most intense in the league.

The '''Lliga de les Graderies''' (Terraces League), which emerged in the late 1950s as an informal competition among the Black community's own teams, was closely connected to CE Sant Bart. Several of the club's Black players competed in the Terraces League when not selected for the first team, and the improvisational style of play that characterised the Terraces League — quick, creative, and suited to the confined spaces of vacant-lot football — would later become the tactical foundation of CE Sant Bart's post-reunification success.

=== Desegregation and the unified league (1960–1973) ===

The [[Twelve Guarantees]] abolished the Reglament de Jugadors and required the desegregation of Balboan football. The impact on CE Sant Bart was immediate and transformative. The club, which had been forced to field a majority-white team under the regulations, was now able to select its best players regardless of race. The Black players who had been confined to the Terraces League or restricted to token appearances flooded into the club, and the result was a rapid improvement in the quality of the squad.

The merger of the two leagues into the unified [[La Lliga de l'Istme]] in 1961 placed CE Sant Bart in direct competition with the corridor clubs for the first time. The early years of the unified league were dominated by the corridor clubs, which had the advantage of better infrastructure, more experienced players, and the Fosian coaching methods introduced during the mandate period. CE Sant Bart, despite its improved squad, struggled to adapt to the higher standard of competition and finished outside the top four in each of the first three unified seasons.

The club's breakthrough came in the 1964 Apertura, when a squad composed largely of former Terraces League players — including the forwards [[Kwasi "El Fletxa" Osei|Kwasi Osei]] and [[Birú people|Birú]] striker [[Yawa Kuri]] — won the club's first unified title. The victory was celebrated across the interior as a vindication of the Black community's sporting talent and a repudiation of the Republic league's racial restrictions. The 1964 Apertura is regarded as the most significant title in the club's history, and the match in which the title was clinched — a 3–1 victory over FC Sant Cristòfor at the Estadi Nacional — is one of the most celebrated in Balboan football.

=== The Clàssic era (1964–1985) ===

The 1964 Apertura inaugurated the golden age of the '''Clàssic de l'Istme'''. Between 1964 and 1985, CE Sant Bart and FC Sant Cristòfor won 20 of the 25 titles contested, and their matches became the central event of the Balboan sporting year. CE Sant Bart won 9 titles in this period, bringing its total to 14, while FC Sant Cristòfor won 11.

The rivalry carried the full weight of the corridor–interior cultural divide. CE Sant Bart drew its support from the interior's working-class communities, while FC Sant Cristòfor represented the corridor's commercial and multilingual population. The matches were charged with the political tensions of the reunification period, and the Estadi Nacional and the Estadi del Port were filled to capacity for every Clàssic.

The club's tactical identity during this period was defined by the '''estil de les graderies''' (Terraces style), a quick, improvisational approach to attacking play that had been developed in the Terraces League and adapted to the formal game by the club's first Black manager, [[Birú people|Birú]] coach [[Arawak Imotu]], who took charge in 1966. The estil de les graderies emphasised close control, rapid passing, and positional interchange, and it contrasted sharply with the more structured, Fosian-influenced style of the corridor clubs. The style was both a tactical choice and a cultural statement: it was the Black community's way of playing, and its success on the national stage was a source of immense pride.

=== Decline and modern era (1986–present) ===

The club's dominance declined after the mid-1980s. The estil de les graderies was increasingly countered by the corridor clubs, which had adopted its principles and combined them with the physical and organisational advantages of the wealthier clubs. The interior's economic stagnation reduced the club's revenue, and the best Black players began to leave for the corridor clubs or for the [[Abyalan Football Confederation|AFC]] leagues abroad. CE Sant Bart won only one title after 1985 — the 2003 Clausura — and spent much of the 1990s and 2000s in the middle of the table.

The 2010s saw a revival under manager [[Amara Diallo]], a former CE Sant Bart player who had been educated in the Terraces League tradition. Diallo rebuilt the club's youth system, re-established the connection with the Terraces League's community football programmes, and introduced a modernised version of the estil de les graderies that incorporated pressing and positional play. The club has not yet won a title under Diallo, but it has finished in the top four in each of the last five seasons and has reached the [[Copa de l'Istme]] final twice.

== Stadium ==

{{main|Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)}}

Club Esportiu Sant Bart has played at the [[Estadi Nacional (Sant Bart)|Estadi Nacional]] since its completion in 1938. The stadium, which was built as a public works project by the rump state, is the largest in Balboa with a capacity of 42,000. It is located in the centre of Sant Bart, on the site of the former plantation workers' housing that was cleared for the construction.

The Estadi Nacional was the home of the '''graderies''' — the open terraces behind the goals where the Black working class stood during the segregated league era. The graderies were the spiritual home of the club's support, and the atmosphere they generated was legendary in Balboan football. The terraces were converted to all-seater configuration in 1998, following the FBF's stadium safety regulations, but the '''Grada de la Terra''' (Terrace of the Earth), the stand behind the north goal, retains the name and the cultural significance of the original graderies.

== Rivalries ==

=== Clàssic de l'Istme ===

{{main|Clàssic de l'Istme}}

The '''Clàssic de l'Istme''' is the rivalry between CE Sant Bart and [[FC Sant Cristòfor]], the two most successful clubs in Balboan football. The rivalry is the most attended fixture in the Balboan game and one of the most politically charged in Lurandian football. The two clubs represent the interior and the canal corridor respectively, and the fixture has historically been associated with the broader cultural and economic divisions between the two regions.

=== Derbi de Jala ===

The '''Derbi de Jala''' is the rivalry between CE Sant Bart and [[Club Deportivo Sant Bart B]], the city's two professional clubs. The derby is less charged than the Clàssic but is nonetheless intensely contested. CD Sant Bart B, which was founded in 1952 and is historically associated with the cooperative farm workers' organisations, has a more explicitly working-class identity than its cross-city rival, and the derby divides the city along lines of class and community organisation.

